  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how proteases in the tumor microenvironment help cancer spread from a primary tumor to distant organs. On the left, a tumor mass is shown surrounded by stromal cells such as fibroblasts, macrophages, and lymphocytes within extracellular matrix, with one cancer cell becoming invasive and entering a blood vessel.\n\nThe main inset explains that proteases promote local invasion by weakening cell–cell junctions, altering integrin-based adhesion, activating other enzymes, signaling through PAR receptors, and degrading the surrounding matrix. Together these changes let cancer cells detach, migrate, and penetrate tissue.\n\nAnother inset shows how proteases support angiogenesis: they release or regulate growth factors and signaling molecules associated with new vessel formation, helping create routes for tumor access to the circulation.\n\nWithin the bloodstream, protease activity appears to aid intravasation, adhesion to the vessel wall, and extravasation, while clusters of platelets around cancer cells suggest protection and support for survival in transit.\n\nAt the bottom, the spread to brain, lung, and bone is illustrated. A final bone-focused panel highlights remodeling of the metastatic niche, where protease-related processing of factors such as IGF/IGFBP and PTHrP-associated signaling appears to stimulate osteoblasts and osteoclasts, favoring bone metastasis.",
